 ## Known limitations and future work
 
-- **The served model changed on 13 and on 19 October; the live validation was not redone.** On 19 October build 8 trained on all 17.28M rows and re-selected the threshold (0.91), v4 F1 0.7291. Until 13 October the served models were trained in July by earlier scripts and adopted with hashes (manifest `git_commit: null`). Since 13 October the served model is the pipeline-trained candidate (MLflow run `f622a7a9…`, v4 F1 0.7278), promoted by `Jenkinsfile.model`. Still open: `scripts/live_k8s_validation.py` has not been rerun against the promoted model, and the live-versus-offline comparison made in this project (precision 0.676, recall 0.788, F1 0.728 over 39,789 matched live predictions) was measured on the July model. The F1 difference (+0.003) is not claimed as an improvement. No deployment file carries the threshold any more: the API reads it from the manifest, so a changed threshold needs no file sync.
+- **The served model changed on 13 and on 19 October; the live validation was rerun only for the latest.** On 19 October build 8 trained on all 17.28M rows and re-selected the threshold (0.91), v4 F1 0.7291. Until 13 October the served models were trained in July by earlier scripts and adopted with hashes (manifest `git_commit: null`). Since 13 October the served model is the pipeline-trained candidate (MLflow run `f622a7a9…`, v4 F1 0.7278), promoted by `Jenkinsfile.model`. `scripts/live_k8s_validation.py` was rerun on 19 October against the promoted model (16,600 requests, 0 errors, see Current production). Still open: the live-versus-offline comparison made earlier in this project (precision 0.676, recall 0.788, F1 0.728 over 39,789 matched live predictions) was measured on the July model and was not repeated. The F1 difference (+0.003) is not claimed as an improvement. No deployment file carries the threshold any more: the API reads it from the manifest, so a changed threshold needs no file sync.
 
 - **Disk headroom is a CI dependency.** Every Jenkins build leaves a ~2 GB image on the host (the registry keeps its own copy, so local images are safe to prune), and SonarQube's Elasticsearch switches every index to read-only when the disk passes 95% (observed live, and again at 97% before a cleanup). Unpruned, a day of builds is enough to reach it. Routine: `docker image prune -a -f --filter "until=24h"` after heavy CI days and a `df -h /` before a demo. The registry volume (about 6 GB, every tag since `2.3.0`) is the one store that only grows; trimming it needs registry garbage collection, which means recreating its container with deletes enabled and its network preserved.
 - **Dependencies are pinned at the top level, not locked.** `requirements-api.txt`, `boto3` and `msgpack` are pinned exactly, but their transitive dependencies are only bounded (`>=`), so two builds hours apart can install different versions (observed: `python-dotenv` 1.2.3 → 1.2.4 and `uvloop` 0.22.1 → 0.23.0 between builds `b93` and `b94`; the image scan reports 0 vulnerabilities in both). A lockfile generated from the image's Python environment would close it; it needs its own verification cycle, so it is deliberately left for after the defense.
